 {"slug": "americas-most-innovative-companies", "title": "America's Most Innovative Companies", "year": 2025, "rank": 178, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2025, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2024, "rank": 276, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500--food-beverages-tobacco", "title": "Fortune Sector Leaders: Food, Beverages, and Tobacco", "year": 2024, "rank": 3, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2024, "rank": 85, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2023, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2023, "rank": 266, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "americas-most-innovative-companies", "title": "America's Most Innovative Companies", "year": 2023, "rank": 182, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2023, "rank": 80, "itemDescription": "Tyson Foods is among the world\u2019s largest meat processors. Based in Springdale, Ark., the company recorded $53 billion in revenue, up 13% from the previous year, and projected a robust 2023.<br />\r\n<ul><br />\r\n \t<li>Rising inflation and supply-chain snarls in 2022 trickled down to food, prompting Tyson to increase prices on some of its meats. Such factors, combined with a tighter consumer budget, led to mixed results across Tyson\u2019s product portfolio. For instance, sales of pork and beef decreased, while chicken sales increased, a sign of consumers opting for cheaper proteins.</li><br />\r\n \t<li>The company\u2019s CFO and fourth-generation heir, John R. Tyson, was arrested in November 2022 and charged with public intoxication and criminal trespassing. He pleaded guilty, apologized, and received the board\u2019s backing to stay in his role at Tyson Foods.</li><br />\r\n \t<li>The company announced plans in December 2022 to consolidate its offices in Illinois and South Dakota and move corporate workers to its headquarters in Arkansas.</li><br />\r\n</ul>"}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2022, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2022, "rank": 292, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2022, "rank": 81, "itemDescription": "<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Tyson Foods is the world\u2019s second-largest meat processor and marketer. The company has struggled with absenteeism at its production facilities due to the pandemic, as well as supply-chain challenges and inflation. Tyson has largely been able to pass those costs through to customers, however, and profit was up substantially in both 2021 and the first quarter of 2022. In June 2021, Tyson announced a goal of achieving net-zero greenhouse gas emissions by 2050; and in 2022, the company invested $60 million toward providing free education for its U.S. employees.</span>"}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2021, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2021, "rank": 270, "itemDescription": "<span data-sheets-value=\"{&quot;1&quot;:2,&quot;2&quot;:&quot;Despite plant closures and mass COVID-19 infections in its workforce, Tyson, the biggest meat producer in the U.S., ended the 2020 fiscal year with net revenues of $43.2 billion, a nearly 2% increase from 2019. After discontinuing a patty made of a mix of real beef and pea protein launched in 2019, Tyson recently debuted an expanded selection of 100% vegan products, including plant-based ground beef, bratwurst, and Italian sausage. The launch comes as the company pushes to gain market share in the plant-based foods market dominated by Beyond Meat and Impossible Foods.&quot;}\" data-sheets-userformat=\"{&quot;2&quot;:14721,&quot;3&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:0},&quot;10&quot;:2,&quot;11&quot;:4,&quot;14&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:2,&quot;2&quot;:0},&quot;15&quot;:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;16&quot;:11}\">Despite plant closures and mass COVID-19 infections in its workforce, Tyson, the biggest meat producer in the U.S., ended the 2020 fiscal year with net revenues of $43.2 billion, a nearly 2% increase from 2019. After discontinuing a patty made of a mix of real beef and pea protein launched in 2019, Tyson recently debuted an expanded selection of 100% vegan products, including plant-based ground beef, bratwurst, and Italian sausage. The launch comes as the company pushes to gain market share in the plant-based foods market dominated by Beyond Meat and Impossible Foods.</span>"}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2021, "rank": 73, "itemDescription": "<span data-sheets-value=\"{&quot;1&quot;:2,&quot;2&quot;:&quot;Despite plant closures and mass COVID-19 infections in its workforce, Tyson, the biggest meat producer in the U.S., ended the 2020 fiscal year with net revenues of $43.2 billion, a nearly 2% increase from 2019. After discontinuing a patty made of a mix of real beef and pea protein launched in 2019, Tyson recently debuted an expanded selection of 100% vegan products, including plant-based ground beef, bratwurst, and Italian sausage. The launch comes as the company pushes to gain market share in the plant-based foods market dominated by Beyond Meat and Impossible Foods.&quot;}\" data-sheets-userformat=\"{&quot;2&quot;:14721,&quot;3&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:0},&quot;10&quot;:2,&quot;11&quot;:4,&quot;14&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:2,&quot;2&quot;:0},&quot;15&quot;:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;16&quot;:11}\">Despite plant closures and mass COVID-19 infections in its workforce, Tyson, the biggest meat producer in the U.S., ended the 2020 fiscal year with net revenues of $43.2 billion, a nearly 2% increase from 2019. After discontinuing a patty made of a mix of real beef and pea protein launched in 2019, Tyson recently debuted an expanded selection of 100% vegan products, including plant-based ground beef, bratwurst, and Italian sausage. The launch comes as the company pushes to gain market share in the plant-based foods market dominated by Beyond Meat and Impossible Foods.</span>"}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2020, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2020, "rank": 287, "itemDescription": "Tyson CEO Noel White, who was tapped for the top spot in September 2018, made good on his promise to expand into international markets in 2019. The Arkansas-based meat giant acquired the Thai and European operations of Brazilian food processor BRF S.A., giving it a scalable footprint in Asia and across Europe via an array of regional brands, like Grabits, Hot \u2018N\u2019 Kickin\u2019 Chicken, Speedy Pollo, and Sadia. The company also launched its Raised &amp; Rooted brand to introduce \u201cvalue-added protein\u201d options, often in highly specialized forms like plant-based nuggets or blended burgers. The company ended 2019 with $42.4 billion in revenue, a 5.8% increase from 2018. April 2020 presented a brand new challenge, as the company shut down plants temporarily as hundreds of workers tested positive for COVID-19."}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2020, "rank": 79, "itemDescription": "Tyson CEO Noel White, who was tapped for the top spot in September 2018, made good on his promise to expand into international markets in 2019. The Arkansas-based meat giant acquired the Thai and European operations of Brazilian food processor BRF S.A., giving it a scalable footprint in Asia and across Europe via an array of regional brands, like Grabits, Hot \u2018N\u2019 Kickin\u2019 Chicken, Speedy Pollo, and Sadia. The company also launched its Raised &amp; Rooted brand to introduce \u201cvalue-added protein\u201d options, often in highly specialized forms like plant-based nuggets or blended burgers. The company ended 2019 with $42.4 billion in revenue, a 5.8% increase from 2018. April 2020 presented a brand new challenge, as the company shut down plants temporarily as hundreds of workers tested positive for COVID-19."}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2019, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2019, "rank": 306, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2019, "rank": 80, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2018, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2018, "rank": 297, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2018, "rank": 80, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "change-the-world", "title": "Change the World", "year": 2018, "rank": 44, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2017, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2017, "rank": 283, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2017, "rank": 82, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2016, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2016, "rank": 235, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2016, "rank": 66, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2015, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "gl